---
source: crates/e2e/tests/walk.rs
expression: text
---
######## 61. Finding which secret holds a key (api)

$ kagebunshin list --keys --prefix acme/dev/ --profile acme-dev
4 matches
  acme/dev/api      aws_sm  API_KEY, API_URL
  acme/dev/db       aws_sm  DATABASE_URL
  acme/dev/queue    aws_sm  QUEUE_URL
  acme/dev/signing  aws_sm  (a plain string, not JSON)

$ kagebunshin search DATABASE_URL --keys --profile acme-dev
3 matches
  acme/dev/db  aws_sm  DATABASE_URL
  blog.dev     file    DATABASE_URL, LOG_LEVEL, PORT, SESSION_SECRET
  shop.dev     file    DATABASE_URL, STRIPE_SECRET

$ kagebunshin project facts
checkout $T/work/api (remotes: github.com/acme/api)
apps:
  the repository root
    .env.example: API_KEY, API_URL, DATABASE_URL, SIGNING_KEY, LOG_LEVEL (has a value)
daemon: unlocked
aws profiles:
  acme-dev: usable, lists 7 secrets
  acme-prod: not checked: it looks like production (has the part prod)
secrets named in the checkout's files:
  acme/dev/db: .github/workflows/deploy-dev.yml
  acme/dev/api: .github/workflows/deploy-dev.yml
  acme/prod/db: .github/workflows/deploy-prod.yml
  acme/prod/api: .github/workflows/deploy-prod.yml
  acme/dev/queue: scripts/seed.sh
secret lists in the workflows, in their order:
  .github/workflows/deploy-dev.yml:4 (environment dev): acme/dev/db, acme/dev/api
  .github/workflows/deploy-prod.yml:11 (environment prod): acme/prod/db, acme/prod/api

$ kagebunshin project draft
draft of project api for the whole repository:
  environment dev, its secrets as .github/workflows/deploy-dev.yml:4 lists them:
    source: aws_sm secrets acme/dev/db, acme/dev/api (profile acme-dev)
    holds: API_KEY, API_URL, DATABASE_URL
    open points (the commands work once it is installed):
    - SIGNING_KEY is in no secret read; secret acme/dev/signing is a plain string whose name is part of that key's, so it may be its value
        kagebunshin project add-source api dev --provider aws_sm --secret acme/dev/signing --key SIGNING_KEY --profile acme-dev   (agent)
    - LOG_LEVEL: no secret holds it, and .env.example gives it a value; only if .env.example's value is the real setting, not a placeholder; a secret belongs in the store or a provider
        kagebunshin project set-var api dev LOG_LEVEL info   (agent)
  environment prod, its secrets as .github/workflows/deploy-prod.yml:11 lists them:
    source: aws_sm secrets acme/prod/db, acme/prod/api (profile acme-dev)
  left out: AWS profile acme-prod: not checked: it looks like production (has the part prod)
  found and unused: secret acme/dev/queue is named in scripts/seed.sh, and no workflow loads it
  to install it: save it as api.toml, then `kagebunshin project apply api.toml`
# Drafted by `kagebunshin project draft`. Check it with
# `kagebunshin project apply FILE --check`.
schema = 1
remotes = ["github.com/acme/api"]
profile = "acme-dev"
# left out: AWS profile acme-prod: not checked: it looks like production (has the part prod)
# found and unused: secret acme/dev/queue is named in scripts/seed.sh, and no workflow loads it

[envs.dev]
# its secrets as .github/workflows/deploy-dev.yml:4 lists them, in that order
# open: SIGNING_KEY is in no secret read; secret acme/dev/signing is a plain string whose name is part of that key's, so it may be its value
#   kagebunshin project add-source api dev --provider aws_sm --secret acme/dev/signing --key SIGNING_KEY --profile acme-dev
# open: LOG_LEVEL: no secret holds it, and .env.example gives it a value; only if .env.example's value is the real setting, not a placeholder; a secret belongs in the store or a provider
#   kagebunshin project set-var api dev LOG_LEVEL info

[[envs.dev.sources]]
provider = "aws_sm"
secrets = ["acme/dev/db", "acme/dev/api"]

[envs.prod]
# its secrets as .github/workflows/deploy-prod.yml:11 lists them, in that order

[[envs.prod.sources]]
provider = "aws_sm"
secrets = ["acme/prod/db", "acme/prod/api"]
